        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.105,
            0.9,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.139,
            0.596,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "Now let us take the case of the area of the segment APB of a circle with centre O and radius r"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.178,
            0.425,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": "(see Fig. 11.4). You can see that :"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.203,
            0.855,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": "Area of the segment APB = Area of the sector OAPB – Area of   OAB"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.399,
            0.228,
            0.697,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\theta}{360} \\times \\pi r^{2} -  area of   \\Delta  OAB",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.274,
            0.775,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "**Note :** From Fig. 11.3 and Fig. 11.4 respectively, you can observe that:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.299,
            0.889,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "Area of the major sector $\\mathrm { O A Q B } = \\pi r ^ { 2 } - \\mathrm { A r e a }$ of the minor sector OAPB and Area of major segment $\\mathrm { A Q B } = \\pi r ^ { 2 }$ – Area of the minor segment APB Let us now take some examples to understand these concepts (or results)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.383,
            0.596,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "**Example 1 :** Find the area of the sector of a circle with radius 4 cm and of angle 30°. Also, find the area of the corresponding major sector (Use   = 3.14)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.446,
            0.578,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Given sector is OAPB (see Fig. 11.5)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.65,
            0.381,
            0.856,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.472,
            0.715,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "2 Area of the sector = ×πr 360 30 ×3.14×4×4cm² 360 12.56 2 2 cm 4.19cm  (approx.) 3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.707,
            0.545,
            0.805,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 11.5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.612,
            0.482,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "Area of the corresponding major sector"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.338,
            0.643,
            0.695,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} &= \\pi r^{2} -  area of sector OAPB  \\\\&= (3.14 \\times 16 - 4.19)  cm ^{2} \\\\&= 46.05  cm ^{2} = 46.1  cm ^{2} ( approach. )\\\\ \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.731,
            0.837,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "**Alternatively,** area of the major se $\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{factor} &= \\frac{(360 - \\theta)}{360} \\times \\pi r^{2} \\\\&= \\left( \\frac{360 - 30}{360} \\right) \\times 3.14 \\times 16 \\operatorname{cm}^{2} \\\\&= \\frac{330}{360} \\times 3.14 \\times 16 \\operatorname{cm}^{2} = 46.05 \\operatorname{cm}^{2} \\\\&= 46.1 \\operatorname{cm}^{2} (\\operatorname{apprcx.})\\end{aligned}$"
        },
        {
